sts."}, {"sourceSlug": "tupac-shakur", "sourceName": "Tupac Shakur", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "rapping", "targetName": "Rapping", "context": "Tupac Shakur's impactful lyrics and performances in the 1990s framed rapping as a powerful medium for social commentary and personal narrative."}], "places": [{"name": "Bronx, United States", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, United States", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Atlanta, United States", "role": "Major Scene"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: rapping", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "rapping"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6010", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapping"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "rapping left a notable mark on Contemporary history, documented across 105 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Rapping has reshaped global music and cultural expression, influencing social movements and identity politics, and it continues to be a vital voice for marginalized communities.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T21:19:41Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27167444875", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Artistic form of vocal delivery and emotive expression that incorporates "rhyme, rhythmic speech, and [commonly] street vernacular" Created c. 1970.", "newValue": "Rapping, originating in the early 1970s primarily within African American communities in the United States, represents a profound evolution in musical expression and cultural storytelling. This form of vocal delivery, characterized by rhythmic speech and intricate rhyme schemes, serves as a powerful vehicle for social commentary, identity, and artistic creativity. Key figures such as Kool Herc, Grandmaster Flash, and later artists like Tupac Shakur and The Notorious B.I.G., have played pivotal roles in shaping its narrative, expanding its reach beyond the streets of the Bronx to a global phenomenon.

The genre's formative years were marked by groundbreaking events, including the first hip-hop parties hosted by DJ Kool Herc in 1973, which laid the groundwork for what would evolve into a multi-billion dollar industry. The 1980s saw the mainstream rise of hip-hop with tracks like "Rapper's Delight" by The Sugarhill Gang, which introduced rapping to a wider audience. Throughout the decades, rapping has been instrumental in addressing issues such as race, poverty, and violence, influencing not only music but also fashion, language, and lifestyle across various cultures.

